# What makes a question causal?
next: {"title":"Why insured people can look sicker: selection bias, decomposed","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/why-insured-people-can-look-sicker-selection-bias-decomposed.cast"}

## Her Two Futures
voice: female
level: basic
use: decision_tree
with:
    root:
      id: maria
      type: decision
      label: Maria, age 40
      children: [{label: Insured (D_i = 1), node: {id: y1, type: terminal, label: Y_i(1), payoff: 72}}, {label: Uninsured (D_i = 0), node: {id: y0, type: terminal, label: Y_i(0), payoff: 64}}]

Governments spend billions on health coverage, and across these four short parts we'll see why proving it helped anyone required a lottery.
    draw node_maria label_maria

Meet Maria: forty, uninsured, one of about ninety thousand Oregonians who signed up in 2008 when the state had money for only ten thousand more adults.
    highlight node_maria effect glow

    pause 1

One road: she gets coverage. D equals one is just a switch — one means insured, zero means not.
    draw edge_maria_y1 branchlabel_maria_y1 node_y1 label_y1

A year later her health scores 72 out of 100. That's Y of one: her health in the world where she's covered.
    draw payoff_y1

    pause 1

The other road: same woman, same year, no coverage — she'd have scored 64. That's Y of zero.
    draw edge_maria_y0 branchlabel_maria_y0 node_y0 label_y0 payoff_y0

    pause 1

Insurance's effect on Maria is the gap between those two numbers: eight points.
    arrow gap_arrow payoff_y0 -> payoff_y1 curved #8a5fa8
    label gap_label "Effect for Maria = 72 − 64 = 8" right #8a5fa8 attach_to gap_arrow

So "insurance improved her health" compares Maria with Maria — not with her insured neighbour, and not with any average.
    focus payoff_y1 payoff_y0

    pause 1

But the world runs only one road. She enrolled, so 72 sits on the record — and 64 was never measured, by anyone, ever.
    mark cross_y0 target payoff_y0 kind cross

The unlived branch isn't merely unrecorded — it never happens, and the gap we wanted vanishes with it.
    erase gap_arrow gap_label edge_maria_y0 branchlabel_maria_y0 node_y0 label_y0 payoff_y0 cross_y0 delivery grave

    pause 1

All the data ever holds is one number per person: whichever future she actually lived.
    text eq_text "Y_i = Y_i(1) if D_i = 1,   Y_i(0) if D_i = 0" x 500 y 100 font_size 26

Her effect stays perfectly well defined — and permanently unobservable.
    text punch_text "Y_i(1) − Y_i(0): defined for her, never observed" #b5482e x 500 y 52 font_size 24

Which is the strange part: the quantity every health policy argument is about has never once been seen for a single patient.
    point at.ref punch_text gesture underline

A causal question asks about two futures for one person — and for that person, the answer is never in the data.
    mark punch_box target punch_text kind box

Next: line up thousands of Marias in a table, and watch one column come up always blank.
    delivery soft

## The Column That Is Always Blank
voice: female
level: basic

    text title "Two columns — one is always blank" x 500 y 690 font_size 30 hidden true
    path t_box closed #8f887c points [[110,320],[890,320],[890,620],[110,620]] hidden true
    path t_hsep #8f887c points [[110,555],[890,555]] hidden true
    path t_r1 #8f887c points [[110,496],[890,496]] hidden true
    path t_r2 #8f887c points [[110,437],[890,437]] hidden true
    path t_r3 #8f887c points [[110,378],[890,378]] hidden true
    path t_v1 #8f887c points [[260,320],[260,620]] hidden true
    path t_v2 #8f887c points [[380,320],[380,620]] hidden true
    path t_v3 #8f887c points [[530,320],[530,620]] hidden true
    path t_v4 #8f887c points [[680,320],[680,620]] hidden true
    text h_person "Person" x 185 y 588 font_size 22 hidden true
    text h_d "Dᵢ" x 320 y 598 font_size 22 hidden true
    text h_d_sub "insured?" x 320 y 570 font_size 18 hidden true
    text h_y1 "Yᵢ(1)" #2f6b8f x 455 y 598 font_size 22 hidden true
    text h_y1_sub "if insured" #2f6b8f x 455 y 570 font_size 18 hidden true
    text h_y0 "Yᵢ(0)" #b5482e x 605 y 598 font_size 22 hidden true
    text h_y0_sub "if not insured" #b5482e x 605 y 570 font_size 18 hidden true
    text h_eff "Yᵢ(1) − Yᵢ(0)" #8a5fa8 x 785 y 598 font_size 22 hidden true
    text h_eff_sub "causal effect" #8a5fa8 x 785 y 570 font_size 18 hidden true

Now that we have seen Maria's two futures, one question remains: why can no study, however large, ever measure what insurance did to her?
    draw title

    draw t_box t_hsep t_r1 t_r2 t_r3 t_v1 t_v2 t_v3 t_v4 parallel true

One row per person, and a column recording whether she had coverage: D equals one for insured, zero for not.
    draw h_person h_d h_d_sub

This column holds her health with insurance — days she felt well, out of thirty — and this one, the very same month lived without coverage.
    draw h_y1 h_y1_sub h_y0 h_y0_sub

    pause 1

Maria won a Medicaid slot in Oregon's lottery, and felt well twenty-four days out of thirty. That number is real; a clerk could look it up.
    text r1_name "Maria" x 185 y 525 font_size 22
    text r1_d "1" x 320 y 525 font_size 22
    text r1_y1 "24" #2f6b8f x 455 y 525 font_size 24

Her other cell stays a question mark, because nobody can rewind the month and take the insurance back off her.
    text r1_y0 "?" #8f887c x 605 y 525 font_size 28

    pause 1

And the quantity we actually came for sits in this last column: her health with coverage, minus her health without it.
    draw h_eff h_eff_sub

Twenty-four minus a question mark is a question mark — Maria's causal effect is not a hard number, it is an unfillable cell.
    text r1_eff "?" #8f887c x 785 y 525 font_size 28

    pause 1

James was never drawn: we see nineteen good days uninsured, and the blank has simply jumped to the other column.
    text r2_name "James" x 185 y 466 font_size 22
    text r2_d "0" x 320 y 466 font_size 22
    text r2_y1 "?" #8f887c x 455 y 466 font_size 28
    text r2_y0 "19" #b5482e x 605 y 466 font_size 24
    text r2_eff "?" #8f887c x 785 y 466 font_size 28

Rosa, Daniel, and the ninety thousand Oregonians behind them: every new row arrives carrying its own fresh blank, and the last column stays empty all the way down.
    text r3_name "Rosa" x 185 y 407 font_size 22
    text r3_d "1" x 320 y 407 font_size 22
    text r3_y1 "27" #2f6b8f x 455 y 407 font_size 24
    text r3_y0 "?" #8f887c x 605 y 407 font_size 28
    text r3_eff "?" #8f887c x 785 y 407 font_size 28
    text r4_name "Daniel" x 185 y 349 font_size 22
    text r4_d "0" x 320 y 349 font_size 22
    text r4_y1 "?" #8f887c x 455 y 349 font_size 28
    text r4_y0 "22" #b5482e x 605 y 349 font_size 24
    text r4_eff "?" #8f887c x 785 y 349 font_size 28

    pause 1

These gaps are not noise — noise shrinks as you collect more data, but a number that was never created stays missing forever.
    focus r1_y0 r2_y1 r3_y0 r4_y1

Jerzy Neyman first wrote these two columns side by side in nineteen twenty-three, in a paper about yields on agricultural plots.
    portrait neyman of "Jerzy Neyman" cameo true

Sixty years later Paul Holland gave the gap the name it still carries.
    erase neyman

The fundamental problem of causal inference: for every person, at least one of the two numbers we need is missing — and no sample size, no clever estimator, can conjure it.
    text note_holland "The fundamental problem of causal inference" x 500 y 268 font_size 24
    mark note_box target note_holland kind box
    delivery grave

    pause 1

    clear keep ["title"]

So Oregon, deciding whether to buy ten thousand Medicaid slots, could never ask what coverage did for Maria herself — that question has no answer in any data set on earth.
    box want_node "Maria's own effect"
    mark want_cross target want_node kind cross

What can be answered is a group question: the average health of people who got coverage, against people who did not.
    box got_node "Average effect in a group"
    arrow link want_node -> got_node curved
    label label_link "borrow the missing half from others" above attach_to link

Missing data, not statistics — causal inference is the craft of filling one person's blank cell with somebody else's.
    highlight got_node effect glow

## From One Person to an Average
voice: female
level: basic

    text cap_units "Health score: 0 = worst, 1 = perfect health" #8f887c x 500 y 700 font_size 20 hidden true
    text hdr_person "Person" x 150 y 630 font_size 22 hidden true
    text hdr_y1 "Y(1): with insurance" #b5482e x 400 y 630 font_size 22 hidden true
    text hdr_y0 "Y(0): without" #2f6b8f x 670 y 630 font_size 22 hidden true
    text hdr_eff "Y(1) − Y(0)" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 630 font_size 22 hidden true
    path rule_top #8f887c points [[80,600],[950,600]] hidden true
    text r_ana "Ana" x 150 y 540 font_size 26 hidden true
    text ana_y1 "0.80" #b5482e x 400 y 540 font_size 26 hidden true
    text ana_y0 "?" #2f6b8f x 670 y 540 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r_ben "Ben" x 150 y 480 font_size 26 hidden true
    text ben_y1 "?" #b5482e x 400 y 480 font_size 26 hidden true
    text ben_y0 "0.55" #2f6b8f x 670 y 480 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r_carla "Carla" x 150 y 420 font_size 26 hidden true
    text carla_y1 "0.72" #b5482e x 400 y 420 font_size 26 hidden true
    text carla_y0 "?" #2f6b8f x 670 y 420 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r_dan "Dan" x 150 y 360 font_size 26 hidden true
    text dan_y1 "?" #b5482e x 400 y 360 font_size 26 hidden true
    text dan_y0 "0.61" #2f6b8f x 670 y 360 font_size 26 hidden true
    text eff_ana "?" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 540 font_size 26 hidden true
    text eff_ben "?" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 480 font_size 26 hidden true
    text eff_carla "?" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 420 font_size 26 hidden true
    text eff_dan "?" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 360 font_size 26 hidden true
    path rule_bot #8f887c points [[80,320],[950,320]] hidden true
    text avg_lab "Average" x 150 y 270 font_size 24 hidden true
    text avg_y1 "0.76" #b5482e x 400 y 270 font_size 28 hidden true
    text avg_y0 "0.58" #2f6b8f x 670 y 270 font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow gap_arrow #8a5fa8 from.x 640 from.y 200 to.x 430 to.y 200 hidden true
    label gap_label "0.76 − 0.58 = 0.18" below #8a5fa8 attach_to gap_arrow hidden true
    box n_pool "About 90,000 sign up" hidden true
    box n_drawn "About 30,000 drawn" hidden true
    box n_not "About 60,000 not drawn" hidden true
    arrow e_drawn n_pool -> n_drawn curved hidden true
    arrow e_not n_pool -> n_not curved hidden true
    label lab_drawn "Their average = Y(1)" below #b5482e attach_to n_drawn hidden true
    label lab_not "Their average stands in for Y(0)" below #2f6b8f attach_to n_not hidden true
    mark ann_key target lab_not kind box hidden true
    text finding "More care used. Clearly less depression. Blood sugar barely moved." #8f887c x 500 y 90 font_size 22 hidden true
    box c_missing "The future that never happened" hidden true
    mark ann_final target c_missing kind circle hidden true
    box d_lottery "Lottery: Oregon" hidden true
    box d_did "Other states: ACA expansions" hidden true
    box d_rd "Just before 65: Medicare" hidden true
    box d_sc "A blend of states: Proposition 99" hidden true
    arrow a_lottery d_lottery -> c_missing hidden true
    arrow a_did d_did -> c_missing hidden true
    arrow a_rd d_rd -> c_missing hidden true
    arrow a_sc d_sc -> c_missing hidden true

Now that we have seen what the lottery draws, one question is still open: how do you put a number on a difference that nobody can ever see?
    draw cap_units hdr_person hdr_y1 hdr_y0 rule_top

Ana has coverage, and her health score with it is 0.80 — her score without coverage is a blank we will never fill in.
    draw r_ana ana_y1 ana_y0

    pause 1

Everyone else is the same story from one side or the other: one number we can see, one future we cannot.
    draw r_ben ben_y1 ben_y0 r_carla carla_y1 carla_y0 r_dan dan_y1 dan_y0

    pause 1

So the column we actually care about — what coverage did for each of them — is question marks all the way down.
    draw hdr_eff eff_ana eff_ben eff_carla eff_dan

    pause 1

Now stop reading across the rows and read down the columns instead: the scores we can see average 0.76 with insurance and 0.58 without.
    draw rule_bot avg_lab avg_y1 avg_y0

Eighteen points — a number we can actually compute, even though not one person's own effect is known.
    draw gap_arrow gap_label

    pause 1

An average needs nobody to appear twice; it only needs one group of people to stand in for another group's missing column.
    highlight avg_y1 avg_y0 effect glow

    pause 1

    clear

Oregon, 2008: the state could afford Medicaid for about ten thousand more adults, and roughly ninety thousand people put their names down.
    draw n_pool

A lottery split that list — about thirty thousand names drawn, the rest not — decided by chance and nothing else.
    draw n_drawn n_not e_drawn e_not

    pause 1

Chance makes the two groups mirror images: same ages, same illnesses, same worries, on average.
    highlight n_drawn n_not effect glow

So the people who lost the draw can stand in for the missing column of the people who won it.
    draw lab_drawn lab_not ann_key

    pause 1

That is why health ministries and insurers pay for lotteries and trials: the drawn group used more care and had clearly less depression, while blood-sugar control barely moved — differences no survey could ever have pinned on insurance alone.
    draw finding

    pause 1

    clear

Every design in the rest of this course is an argument about one blank: the future that never happened.
    draw c_missing

A lottery fills it with the people who were not drawn.
    draw d_lottery a_lottery

Difference-in-differences fills it with states that did not expand Medicaid; a birthday cut-off fills it with people a few months short of sixty-five.
    draw d_did a_did d_rd a_rd

Synthetic control builds it out of a weighted blend of other states, mixed to match the one we care about.
    draw d_sc a_sc

    pause 1

One person's effect can never be observed — but an average can, whenever we find an honest stand-in for the future that never happened.
    draw ann_final delivery grave

